$\begin{align}
\left(a^5\right)^{2}&=a^{5\cdot 2}
\\\\
&=a^{10}
\end{align}$

This follows from the general rule $\left(x^m\right)^{n}=x^{m\cdot n}$.

We can also see this is correct by expanding the powers.

$\begin{align}
\left(a^5\right)^{2}&=\underbrace{a^5\cdot a^5}_\text{2 times}
\\\\\\
&=\underbrace{
\underbrace{a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a}_\text{5 times}
\cdot \underbrace{a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a}_\text{5 times}}
_\text{2 times}
\\\\
&=a^{10}
\end{align}$
